## Food

There is no special diet for type 1 diabetes. You can eat most foods, as long as you match your insulin to what you eat. A **healthy, balanced diet** is good for your heart and overall health. A **dietitian** can help you with carbohydrate counting.

## Exercise

Exercise is good for you, but it can lower your glucose, sometimes for hours afterwards. Check before, during and after, and carry fast-acting sugar. Your team can help you plan insulin and food around exercise.

## Alcohol

Alcohol can cause hypos, including the next morning. Eat when you drink, check your glucose before bed, and make sure the people you are with know you have diabetes.

## Driving

If you take insulin, you **must tell the DVLA** and meet the rules, including **checking your glucose before driving** and every 2 hours on long journeys. Keep fast-acting sugar in the car. If your glucose is 5 mmol/L or less, have a snack before you drive. Do not drive if it is below 4 mmol/L or you feel hypo. See GOV.UK for the full rules.

## Travel

Take **twice as much** insulin and supplies as you think you need, and keep them in your hand luggage. Get a letter from your GP or team, and check travel insurance covers your diabetes.

## Diabetes distress

Managing diabetes all day, every day can be exhausting. Feeling overwhelmed, frustrated or burnt out is common. It is called **diabetes distress**. Talk to your diabetes team, who can refer you for support. Diabetes UK runs a free helpline.

## Children and teenagers

If your child has type 1 diabetes, their school must have an **individual healthcare plan** and support them to manage it safely. Diabetes UK and Breakthrough T1D have guides for families.
